===persian===

به رویم در نیستی تو
 ز پهلویم نرستی تو
 چو غم در دل نشستی تو
 نمی دانم چه هستی تو.

نه از آبی نه از خاکی
 نه از طوفان و کولاکی
 به دل آوای غمناکی
 نمی دانم چه هستی تو.

به بام آسمان برفی
 درون قلب شنگرفی
 به چشم اشک و
 به لب حرفی
 نمی دانم چه هستی تو.

تو چون گلهای بادامی
 شقایق های بر بامی
 طنینی از دل جامی
 نمیدانم چه هستی تو.

تو ناقوس کلیسایی
 از آن مغرب مایی

به هر قلبی یک آوایی
 نمی دانم چه هستی تو

پرستوی بهار من
 اميد من قرار من
 ز خود راه فرار من
 نمیدانم چه هستی تو.

به هر راهی تو پوینده
 ز هر دردی تو جوینده
 به هر لفظی تو گوینده
 نمیدانم چه هستی تو.

به جز راهت نمی پویم
 به جز وصلت نمیجویم
 اگر چه فاش میگویم
 نمیدانم چه هستی تو.

بروجرد ۱۳۳۸

===machine===

You are not in front of me, you have not come to my side, you have sat in my heart like sorrow, I do not know what you are.
Not from water, not from earth, not from storm and blizzard, in the heart of a sad voice, I do not know what you are.
On the roof of the snowy sky, in the heart of a thorn, with tears in my eyes and words on my lips, I do not know what you are.
You are like almond flowers, poppies on the roof, resounding from the heart of a cup, I do not know what you are.

You are a church bell from our west.
I don't know what you are with every heart, a single sound

My spring swallow, my hope, my destiny, my escape from myself, I don't know what you are.
You are the one who follows every path, you are the one who seeks every pain, you are the one who speaks every word, I don't know what you are.
I don't follow except your path, I don't seek except union, even though I say it openly, I don't know what you are.

Borujerd 1338

===lantern===

You are not there before my eyes,
you did not grow from my own side,
yet like grief you've settled in my heart —
I do not know what you are.

Not of water, not of earth,
not of the storm or the blizzard;
a mournful voice within the heart —
I do not know what you are.

Snow on the roof of the sky,
vermilion deep in the heart,
a tear in the eye, a word on the lip —
I do not know what you are.

You are like the almond blossoms,
poppies along a rooftop,
a ringing from the heart of a cup —
I do not know what you are.

You are a church bell
out of that West of ours,
a single voice in every heart —
I do not know what you are.

My swallow of spring,
my hope, my one repose,
my road of escape from myself —
I do not know what you are.

On every road, you are the one who strides;
out of every sorrow, the one who searches;
in every word, the one who speaks —
I do not know what you are.

I walk no road but yours,
I seek nothing but your embrace,
and though I say it plainly —
I do not know what you are.

Borujerd, 1338

